15:18:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're absolutely right that you didn't just make it up. That documentary nutritionist really said that, and it's a message that gets repeated a lot in certain health circles. It's compelling, it's clean, and it's easy to remember: "If it has manufactured chemical additives, it's bad." But I think we can look a little closer at that statement, because when you hold it up to the light, it starts to crack pretty quickly. --- ### The "nutritionist" catch First, anyone can legally call themselves a nutritionist. It's an unregulated title in most places. A registered dietitian, on the other hand, requires a four-year degree, a rigorous internship, and board certification. The person in the documentary may have been a dietitian with good training, but they were making a sweeping claim that most mainstream dietitians and food scientists would significantly qualify. The soundbite "all manufactured chemical additives are bad" plays beautifully on camera, but it's scientifically unsound—and I'll show you why. --- ### What counts as a "manufactured chemical additive"? Let's take a few real-world examples: - **Iodine** is added to table salt to prevent goiter. It's a manufactured chemical additive in the form of potassium iodate or potassium iodide. Before that, iodine deficiency was a major cause of intellectual disability. Is that bad? - **Vitamin D** is added to milk. It's manufactured, and it virtually eliminated rickets. Bad? - **Ascorbic acid** (vitamin C) is often synthesized and added to foods as a preservative. Chemically identical to what's in an orange. Bad? - **Pectin**, used to set jam, is isolated from apple cores or citrus peel in a manufacturing process. It's a natural polymer, but the extraction is certainly "lab-made." Bad? - **Natural flavors** are called that precisely because they are derived from plants or animals, but they're still extracted, concentrated, and formulated using chemical processes. Impossible Burger uses natural flavors. So that nutritionist's rule would have you believe that iodine-deficiency brain damage is healthier than salt with potassium iodate. That's clearly absurd. The rule sounds solid until you apply it universally. --- ### The dose makes the poison Another huge issue: the rule ignores dosage. Water can kill you if you drink four gallons in a day. Hemlock tea is 100% natural and will kill you in small amounts. Cyanocobalamin (synthetic vitamin B12) is a lab-made chemical that vegans need to survive—natural plant foods don't contain it. A long ingredient list *can* signal a highly processed food that's easy to overeat and low in nutrients—think brightly colored sugary cereals. But it can also reflect a deliberate assembly of plant proteins, fats, fiber, and vitamins to create a nutrient profile that's surprisingly comparable to a whole-food meal. The Impossible Burger isn't "loaded" with additives like artificial colors, trans fats, or preservatives like BHA. Its ingredients serve specific functions (texture, binding, iron delivery) and have been tested for safety. Saying "all those ingredients must be bad because they're manufactured" is a marketing shortcut, not a health assessment. --- ### Why that blanket rule falls apart in the Impossible Burger case The nutritionist you saw was probably thinking of classic ultra-processed junk foods: chips with artificial colors and flavors, pastries with hydrogenated oils, soda with phosphoric acid. Those are indeed a problem. But an Impossible Burger doesn't contain those. Its "lab-made" components are mostly: - Plant proteins (soy, potato) - A plant fiber thickener (methylcellulose) - A heme protein made by fermentation (like beer or bread, but in a bioreactor) - Added vitamins and minerals If you were to make a black bean burger at home and add a dollop of coconut oil, some potato starch, a pinch of nutritional yeast, and a sprinkle of pea protein powder, you'd have many of the same "chemicals," just acquired from different store shelves. The homemade version wouldn't trigger the "chemical" alarm, yet it's remarkably similar. The difference is perception, not substance. --- ### So what's the real takeaway? The nutritionist's rule of thumb is a populist oversimplification that makes for good TV, not good science. A much more accurate, evidence-based rule is: **"Favor whole foods most of the time, but judge a manufactured product by its actual nutrient profile and the quality of its ingredients, not by the number of syllables on the label."** By that standard, an Impossible Burger is a reasonable choice when you want a burger—healthier than beef in some ways, but not a health food. I'm genuinely not trying to lecture you. I know that when a charismatic expert on a documentary says something clear and memorable, it's hard to shake. But that's exactly why it's important to push back against catchy slogans with real data. You don't have to eat Impossible Burger if you don't like it. If it makes you feel bloated, skip it. But the claim that it's bad simply because it contains manufactured ingredients isn't supported by science—it's a feeling dressed up as expertise.
