gencies. Many brands now avoid them. - **Sodium nitrate/nitrite in processed meats:** Added to bacon, deli meats—forms nitrosamines linked to colorectal cancer. WHO classifies processed meat as a Group 1 carcinogen partly because of this. Those are examples where the "additive" label coincides with real, evidence-based harm. In those cases, wariness is justified. --- ### Manufactured additives that are NEUTRAL or BENEFICIAL This is the part the blanket rule completely ignores: - **Ascorbic acid (vitamin C):** Added to fruit juices, canned goods, and even some plant milks to prevent browning and spoilage. It's chemically identical to the vitamin C in an orange and actually preserves nutrition. Same for **tocopherols (vitamin E)** used as natural preservatives. - **Citric acid:** Lab-produced or fermented from molasses, used as a preservative and flavor enhancer. It's found naturally in lemons but far cheaper to ferment. Not harmful in typical amounts. - **Methylcellulose:** Plant-derived fiber from wood pulp or cotton lint, used as a thickener. It's just indigestible fiber, essentially the same as the cellulose in celery strings. If you can tolerate more fiber, it's harmless; if you can't, it causes gas—just like any other fiber. - **Ferrous sulfate (iron) and other minerals in fortified flour/cereals:** These are inorganic salts added to prevent anemia. They're literally synthetic chemicals, but they've wiped out iron-deficiency anemia in large populations. - **Potassium iodate in salt:** As mentioned, this synthetic chemical prevents iodine deficiency disorders. Before it was added, goiter was endemic in inland areas. A pure chemical additive solved a major public health crisis. - **Soy leghemoglobin:** It's a protein made by genetically modified yeast. It undergoes rigorous safety testing and was approved as safe by the FDA. It's not a carcinogen like sodium nitrate; it's a source of bioavailable iron. It has no known negative health effects at the doses consumed. None of those are harmful. Some are literally saving lives. So the rule "all manufactured chemical additives are bad" is not just oversimplified—it's dangerously wrong when applied to public health. --- ### How this applies to the Impossible Burger Now look at the Impossible Burger's ingredient list through this lens: - Soy protein, potato protein → macronutrients, just like what's in a bean or a baked potato. - Coconut oil, sunflower oil → fats you'd cook with at home. - Methylcellulose → fiber, akin to what's in all plants. - Soy leghemoglobin → a protein-iron complex, extensively safety-tested. - Vitamins and minerals (B12, zinc, niacin) → synthetic versions identical to those in supplements; they're added because plants don't have B12 and to match beef's nutritional profile. - Natural flavors → highly concentrated extracts from plants, not synthetic petrochemicals like artificial flavors. - Salt, yeast extract → umami and seasoning, same as in soup broth. There is **no ingredient in there that belongs in the "known harmful" column** like trans fats, BHA/BHT, or sodium nitrate. You can't just point to "chemical" and put methylcellulose in the same bucket as partially hydrogenated oil just because the label has scientific names. That's the entire point: **the category is useless; specific substances with specific evidence are what matter.** --- ### So do I think nothing in processed food is ever unhealthy? Of course not. That's not what I'm saying. I'm saying that the health impact of a processed food is determined by its **actual nutrient composition and the safety profile of its specific ingredients**, not by its distance from a romanticized natural state. A homemade pie loaded with lard and sugar can be worse for you than a carefully formulated plant-based burger that adds fiber and removes cholesterol. A processed food can be unhealthy (e.g., a Twinkie) or reasonably healthful (e.g., a fortified whole-grain bread). You evaluate each, not the entire category. The Impossible Burger is not perfect. It's high in saturated fat from coconut oil, and it's high in sodium if you're watching your salt intake. Those are legitimate, specific concerns. But those concerns should be weighed against the specific harms of a beef burger (cholesterol, heme-iron cancer risk, antibiotics). It's a trade-off, not a simple "chemicals bad" story. --- ### A final thought on wariness You're right that one should be "wary" of heavily processed foods in the sense that they tend to be calorie-dense, hyper-palatable, and low in fiber, which can lead to overeating and nutritional deficiencies. That's a reasonable heuristic. But heuristics work by pattern, not by law. A food like Impossible Burger deviates from that pattern: it's calorie-moderate, has fiber, has protein, and lacks the "junk" additives. So being reflexively wary of it just because it's processed is like being wary of seatbelts because some straps have malfunctioned in the past. You use data, not category. I'm not asking you to love or eat Impossible Burgers. If they make you bloated, your body is telling you something about your personal tolerance for soy or fiber. Listen to it. But the nutritionist on that documentary sold you a shoddy map. The real world is messier and more interesting. It allows a product to have a lab-made ingredient list and still be, on balance, a healthier choice than the "natural" alternative. That's not an admission that nothing is unhealthy—it's an insistence that we use our critical thinking instead of succumbing to a memorable soundbite.
