e being practically useful today, it might be: **"If it comes from a plant, eat it. If it was made in a plant, look closer—but don't just check the syllables, check what it actually is."** Another one: **"Prioritize foods with short ingredient lists made of things you could in theory grow or raise, but don't be afraid of a lab when it's delivering real nutrition without the health baggage of the original product."** Your grandma wasn't wrong to trust tradition; she was using the best available tool for her time. You have the same right to be skeptical, but you also have access to more information than she did. You can learn that methylcellulose is harmless fiber, that soy leghemoglobin is just a protein carrying iron, and that an Impossible Burger, while imperfect, might actually be a better choice than a beef burger if you're concerned about heart disease and cancer. Or you can choose to eat whole lentils and brown rice instead—and that's even better, honestly. But you get to make that
